see User Questions: http://scikit-learn.org/stable/support.html#user-questions\r\n-->\r\n\r\n<!-- Instructions For Filing a Bug: https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#filing-bugs -->\r\n\r\n#### Description\r\n<!-- Example: Joblib Error thrown when calling fit on LatentDirichletAllocation with evaluate_every > 0-->\r\n@GaelVaroquaux Maybe an online version of the NMF could be useful in big data settings.\r\n\r\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\r\n<!--\r\nExample:\r\n```python\r\nfrom sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer\r\nfrom sklearn.decomposition import LatentDirichletAllocation\r\n\r\ndocs = [\"Help I have a bug\" for i in range(1000)]\r\n\r\nvectorizer = CountVectorizer(input=docs, analyzer='word')\r\nlda_features = vectorizer.fit_transform(docs)\r\n\r\nlda_model = LatentDirichletAllocation(\r\n    n_topics=10,\r\n    learning_method='online',\r\n    evaluate_every=10,\r\n    n_jobs=4,\r\n)\r\nmodel = lda_model.fit(lda_features)\r\n```\r\nIf the code is too long, feel free to put it in a public gist and link\r\nit in the issue: https://gist.github.com\r\n-->\r\n\r\n#### Expected Results\r\n<!-- Example: No error is thrown. Please paste or describe the expected results.-->\r\n\r\n#### Actual Results\r\n<!-- Please paste or specifically describe the actual output or traceback. -->\r\nHere I show the results for an sparse matrix as input of size 1M x 2.8k\r\nThe factorization has `n_components = 10` and each point is an entire pass on the data.\r\nThe current online implementation only supports the kullback-leibler divergence, but in practice\r\ncan be generalized to any beta divergence.\r\n\r\n![batch_vs_online_nmf](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/11759948/53494592-69748b00-3a9e-11e9-8cc8-ce53ce3fe7f2.png)\r\n\r\n#### Versions\r\n<!--\r\nPlease run the following snippet and paste the output below.\r\nFor scikit-learn >= 0.20:\r\nimport sklearn; sklearn.show_versions()\r\nFor scikit-learn < 0.20:\r\nimport platform; print(platform.platform())\r\nimport sys; print(\"Python\", sys.version)\r\nimport numpy; print(\"NumPy\", numpy.__version__)\r\nimport scipy; print(\"SciPy\", scipy.__version__)\r\nimport sklearn; print(\"Scikit-Learn\", sklearn.__version__)\r\n-->\r\n\r\n\r\n<!-- Thanks for contributing! -->\r\n\nDraft implementation of MiniBatchNMF\n<!--\r\nThanks for contributing a pull request! Please ensure you have taken a look at\r\nthe contribution guidelines: https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/blob/master/CONTRIBUTING.md#pull-request-checklist\r\n-->\r\n\r\n#### Reference Issues/PRs\r\n<!--\r\nExample: Fixes #1234. See also #3456.\r\nPlease use keywords (e.g., Fixes) to create link to the issues or pull requests\r\nyou resolved, so that they will automatically be closed when your pull request\r\nis merged. See https://github.com/blog/1506-closing-issues-via-pull-requests\r\n-->\r\n\r\n\r\n#### What does this implement/fix? Explain your changes.\r\n\r\nImplementation of a mini-batch NMF with multiplicative updates.\r\n#13308 \r\n#### Any other comments?\r\n\r\n\r\n<!--\r\nPlease be aware that we are a loose team of volunteers so patience is\r\nnecessary; assistance handling other issues is very welcome. We value\r\nall user contributions, no matter how minor they are. If we are slow to\r\nreview, either the pull request needs some benchmarking, tinkering,\r\nconvincing, etc. or more likely the reviewers are simply busy. In either\r\ncase, we ask for your understanding during the review process.\r\nFor more information, see our FAQ on this topic:\r\nhttp://scikit-learn.org/dev/faq.html#why-is-my-pull-request-not-getting-any-attention.\r\n\r\nThanks for contributing!\r\n-->\n--- END PROBLEM STATEMENT ---\n\nIMPORTANT: You should ONLY interact with the environment provided to you AND NEVER ASK FOR HUMAN HELP.\nDon't include any lambda functions!\nYou should NOT modify any files!\n"}, {"content": "To locate the relevant parts of the scikit-learn codebase for implementing an online version of Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF), we'll start by identifying the key terms and modules mentioned in the issue description and then explore the dependencies.\n\nFirst, let's search for mentions of NMF and related concepts in the codebase:\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>\n[\"Non-negative Matrix Factorization\", \"NMF\", \"MiniBatchNMF\", \"beta divergence\", \"online implementation\"]\n</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nbenchmarks/bench_plot_nmf.py:_nls_subproblem\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_fit_multiplicative_update\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_fit_coordinate_descent\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:non_negative_factorization\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_initialize_nmf\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_psd_eigenvalues\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_check_psd_eigenvalues\nsklearn/utils/validation.py:_generate_get_feature_names_out\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression.__init__\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression.fit\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical.__init__\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSCanonical\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:CCA\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:CCA.__init__\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSRegression\nsklearn/datasets/_arff_parser.py:_sparse_data_to_array\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD.transform\nsklearn/cross_decomposition/_pls.py:PLSSVD\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_beta_divergence\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_beta_divergence\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_fit_multiplicative_update\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF\nsklearn/covariance/_shrunk_covariance.py:ledoit_wolf_shrinkage\nsklearn/metrics/_classification.py:fbeta_score\nsklearn/manifold/_t_sne.py:_kl_divergence\nsklearn/decomposition/_dict_learning.py:MiniBatchDictionaryLearning._update_inner_stats\nsklearn/metrics/cluster/_supervised.py:homogeneity_completeness_v_measure\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation._em_step\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation\nbenchmarks/bench_online_ocsvm.py:impl:190\nsklearn/decomposition/_dict_learning.py:_update_dict\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation._check_params\nsklearn/decomposition/_lda.py:LatentDirichletAllocation.fit\nsklearn/naive_bayes.py:GaussianNB\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_fit_multiplicative_update\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:non_negative_factorization\nbenchmarks/bench_plot_nmf.py:_nls_subproblem\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_beta_divergence\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_beta_divergence\nsklearn/metrics/_classification.py:fbeta_score\nsklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_fit_coordinate_descent\n##Searching for term \"Non-negative Matrix Factorization\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound code snippet in file `examples/applications/plot_topics_extraction_with_nmf_lda.py`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n  1 | \"\"\"\n  2 | =======================================================================================\n  3 | Topic extraction with Non-negative Matrix Factorization and Latent Dirichlet Allocation\n  4 | =======================================================================================\n  5 | \n  6 | This is an example of applying :class:`~sklearn.decomposition.NMF` and\n  7 | :class:`~sklearn.decomposition.LatentDirichletAllocation` on a corpus\n  8 | of documents and extract additive models of the topic structure of the\n  9 | corpus.  The output is a plot of topics, each represented as bar plot\n 10 | using top few words based on weights.\n 11 | \n 12 | Non-negative Matrix Factorization is applied with two different objective\n 13 | functions: the Frobenius norm, and the generalized Kullback-Leibler divergence.\n 14 | The latter is equivalent to Probabilistic Latent Semantic Indexing.\n 15 | \n 16 | The default parameters (n_samples / n_features / n_components) should make\n 17 | the example runnable in a couple of tens of seconds. You can try to\n 18 | increase the dimensions of the problem, but be aware that the time\n 19 | complexity is polynomial in NMF. In LDA, the time complexity is\n 20 | proportional to (n_samples * iterations).\n 21 | \n 22 | \"\"\"\n 23 | \n 24 | # Author: Olivier Grisel <olivier.grisel@ensta.org>\n 25 | #         Lars Buitinck\n 26 | #         Chyi-Kwei Yau <chyikwei.yau@gmail.com>\n 27 | # License: BSD 3 clause\n 28 | \n 29 | from time import time\n 30 | import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n 31 | \n 32 | from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer, CountVectorizer\n 33 | from sklearn.decomposition import NMF, LatentDirichletAllocation\n 34 | from sklearn.datasets import fetch_20newsgroups\n 35 | \n 36 | n_samples = 2000\n 37 | n_features = 1000\n 38 | n_components = 10\n 39 | n_top_words = 20\n```\n\nFound class `sklearn/feature_extraction/text.py:HashingVectorizer`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass HashingVectorizer(TransformerMixin, _VectorizerMixin, BaseEstimator):\n\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        *,\n        input=\"content\",\n        encoding=\"utf-8\",\n        decode_error=\"strict\",\n        strip_accents=None,\n        lowercase=True,\n        preprocessor=None,\n        tokenizer=None,\n        stop_words=None,\n        token_pattern=r\"(?u)\\b\\w\\w+\\b\",\n        ngram_range=(1, 1),\n        analyzer=\"word\",\n        n_features=(2**20),\n        binary=False,\n        norm=\"l2\",\n        alternate_sign=True,\n        dtype=np.float64,\n    ):\n...\n    def partial_fit(self, X, y=None):\n...\n    def fit(self, X, y=None):\n...\n    def transform(self, X):\n...\n    def fit_transform(self, X, y=None):\n...\n    def _get_hasher(self):\n...\n    def _more_tags(self):\n\n```\nHint: Search `sklearn/feature_extraction/text.py:HashingVectorizer` to get the full content if needed.\n\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_fit_coordinate_descent`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n417 | def _fit_coordinate_descent(\n418 |     X,\n419 |     W,\n420 |     H,\n421 |     tol=1e-4,\n422 |     max_iter=200,\n423 |     l1_reg_W=0,\n424 |     l1_reg_H=0,\n425 |     l2_reg_W=0,\n426 |     l2_reg_H=0,\n427 |     update_H=True,\n428 |     verbose=0,\n429 |     shuffle=False,\n430 |     random_state=None,\n431 | ):\n432 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF) with Coordinate Descent\n433 | \n434 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n435 |     and H. Each minimization is done with a cyclic (up to a permutation of the\n436 |     features) Coordinate Descent.\n437 | \n438 |     Parameters\n439 |     ----------\n440 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n441 |         Constant matrix.\n442 | \n443 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n444 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n445 | \n446 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n447 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n448 | \n449 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n450 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n451 | \n452 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n453 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n454 | \n455 |     l1_reg_W : float, default=0.\n456 |         L1 regularization parameter for W.\n457 | \n458 |     l1_reg_H : float, default=0.\n459 |         L1 regularization parameter for H.\n460 | \n461 |     l2_reg_W : float, default=0.\n462 |         L2 regularization parameter for W.\n463 | \n464 |     l2_reg_H : float, default=0.\n465 |         L2 regularization parameter for H.\n466 | \n467 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n468 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n469 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n470 | \n471 |     verbose : int, default=0\n472 |         The verbosity level.\n473 | \n474 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n475 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n476 | \n477 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n478 |         Used to randomize the coordinates in the CD solver, when\n479 |         ``shuffle`` is set to ``True``. Pass an int for reproducible\n480 |         results across multiple function calls.\n481 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n482 | \n483 |     Returns\n484 |     -------\n485 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n486 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n487 | \n488 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n489 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n490 | \n491 |     n_iter : int\n492 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n493 | \n494 |     References\n495 |     ----------\n496 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and Phan, Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n497 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n498 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n499 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n500 |     \"\"\"\n501 |     # so W and Ht are both in C order in memory\n502 |     Ht = check_array(H.T, order=\"C\")\n503 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=\"csr\")\n504 | \n505 |     rng = check_random_state(random_state)\n506 | \n507 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n508 |         violation = 0.0\n509 | \n510 |         # Update W\n511 |         violation += _update_coordinate_descent(\n512 |             X, W, Ht, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, shuffle, rng\n513 |         )\n514 |         # Update H\n515 |         if update_H:\n516 |             violation += _update_coordinate_descent(\n517 |                 X.T, Ht, W, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, shuffle, rng\n518 |             )\n519 | \n520 |         if n_iter == 1:\n521 |             violation_init = violation\n522 | \n523 |         if violation_init == 0:\n524 |             break\n525 | \n526 |         if verbose:\n527 |             print(\"violation:\", violation / violation_init)\n528 | \n529 |         if violation / violation_init <= tol:\n530 |             if verbose:\n531 |                 print(\"Converged at iteration\", n_iter + 1)\n532 |             break\n533 | \n534 |     return W, Ht.T, n_iter\n```\n\nFound function `sklearn/feature_extraction/text.py:HashingVectorizer.fit`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n784 | def fit(self, X, y=None):\n785 |         \"\"\"No-op: this transformer is stateless.\n786 | \n787 |         Parameters\n788 |         ----------\n789 |         X : ndarray of shape [n_samples, n_features]\n790 |             Training data.\n791 | \n792 |         y : Ignored\n793 |             Not used, present for API consistency by convention.\n794 | \n795 |         Returns\n796 |         -------\n797 |         self : object\n798 |             HashingVectorizer instance.\n799 |         \"\"\"\n800 |         # triggers a parameter validation\n801 |         if isinstance(X, str):\n802 |             raise ValueError(\n803 |                 \"Iterable over raw text documents expected, string object received.\"\n804 |             )\n805 | \n806 |         self._warn_for_unused_params()\n807 |         self._validate_params()\n808 | \n809 |         self._get_hasher().fit(X, y=y)\n810 |         return self\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"Non-negative Matrix Factorization\", \"Non-negative Matrix Factorization MiniBatchNMF beta divergence online implementation\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:non_negative_factorization`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 874 | def non_negative_factorization(\n 875 |     X,\n 876 |     W=None,\n 877 |     H=None,\n 878 |     n_components=None,\n 879 |     *,\n 880 |     init=None,\n 881 |     update_H=True,\n 882 |     solver=\"cd\",\n 883 |     beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n 884 |     tol=1e-4,\n 885 |     max_iter=200,\n 886 |     alpha=\"deprecated\",\n 887 |     alpha_W=0.0,\n 888 |     alpha_H=\"same\",\n 889 |     l1_ratio=0.0,\n 890 |     regularization=\"deprecated\",\n 891 |     random_state=None,\n 892 |     verbose=0,\n 893 |     shuffle=False,\n 894 | ):\n 895 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF).\n 896 | \n 897 |     Find two non-negative matrices (W, H) whose product approximates the non-\n 898 |     negative matrix X. This factorization can be used for example for\n 899 |     dimensionality reduction, source separation or topic extraction.\n 900 | \n 901 |     The objective function is:\n 902 | \n 903 |         .. math::\n 904 | \n 905 |             0.5 * ||X - WH||_{loss}^2\n 906 | \n 907 |             + alpha\\\\_W * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_features * ||vec(W)||_1\n 908 | \n 909 |             + alpha\\\\_H * l1_{ratio} * n\\\\_samples * ||vec(H)||_1\n 910 | \n 911 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_W * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_features * ||W||_{Fro}^2\n 912 | \n 913 |             + 0.5 * alpha\\\\_H * (1 - l1_{ratio}) * n\\\\_samples * ||H||_{Fro}^2\n 914 | \n 915 |     Where:\n 916 | \n 917 |     :math:`||A||_{Fro}^2 = \\\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}^2` (Frobenius norm)\n 918 | \n 919 |     :math:`||vec(A)||_1 = \\\\sum_{i,j} abs(A_{ij})` (Elementwise L1 norm)\n 920 | \n 921 |     The generic norm :math:`||X - WH||_{loss}^2` may represent\n 922 |     the Frobenius norm or another supported beta-divergence loss.\n 923 |     The choice between options is controlled by the `beta_loss` parameter.\n 924 | \n 925 |     The regularization terms are scaled by `n_features` for `W` and by `n_samples` for\n 926 |     `H` to keep their impact balanced with respect to one another and to the data fit\n 927 |     term as independent as possible of the size `n_samples` of the training set.\n 928 | \n 929 |     The objective function is minimized with an alternating minimization of W\n 930 |     and H. If H is given and update_H=False, it solves for W only.\n 931 | \n 932 |     Note that the transformed data is named W and the components matrix is named H. In\n 933 |     the NMF literature, the naming convention is usually the opposite since the data\n 934 |     matrix X is transposed.\n 935 | \n 936 |     Parameters\n 937 |     ----------\n 938 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 939 |         Constant matrix.\n 940 | \n 941 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components), default=None\n 942 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 943 | \n 944 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features), default=None\n 945 |         If init='custom', it is used as initial guess for the solution.\n 946 |         If update_H=False, it is used as a constant, to solve for W only.\n 947 | \n 948 |     n_components : int, default=None\n 949 |         Number of components, if n_components is not set all features\n 950 |         are kept.\n 951 | \n 952 |     init : {'random', 'nndsvd', 'nndsvda', 'nndsvdar', 'custom'}, default=None\n 953 |         Method used to initialize the procedure.\n 954 | \n 955 |         Valid options:\n 956 | \n 957 |         - None: 'nndsvda' if n_components < n_features, otherwise 'random'.\n 958 | \n 959 |         - 'random': non-negative random matrices, scaled with:\n 960 |             sqrt(X.mean() / n_components)\n 961 | \n 962 |         - 'nndsvd': Nonnegative Double Singular Value Decomposition (NNDSVD)\n 963 |             initialization (better for sparseness)\n 964 | \n 965 |         - 'nndsvda': NNDSVD with zeros filled with the average of X\n 966 |             (better when sparsity is not desired)\n 967 | \n 968 |         - 'nndsvdar': NNDSVD with zeros filled with small random values\n 969 |             (generally faster, less accurate alternative to NNDSVDa\n 970 |             for when sparsity is not desired)\n 971 | \n 972 |         - 'custom': use custom matrices W and H if `update_H=True`. If\n 973 |           `update_H=False`, then only custom matrix H is used.\n 974 | \n 975 |         .. versionchanged:: 0.23\n 976 |             The default value of `init` changed from 'random' to None in 0.23.\n 977 | \n 978 |         .. versionchanged:: 1.1\n 979 |             When `init=None` and n_components is less than n_samples and n_features\n 980 |             defaults to `nndsvda` instead of `nndsvd`.\n 981 | \n 982 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n 983 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n 984 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n 985 | \n 986 |     solver : {'cd', 'mu'}, default='cd'\n 987 |         Numerical solver to use:\n 988 | \n 989 |         - 'cd' is a Coordinate Descent solver that uses Fast Hierarchical\n 990 |             Alternating Least Squares (Fast HALS).\n 991 | \n 992 |         - 'mu' is a Multiplicative Update solver.\n 993 | \n 994 |         .. versionadded:: 0.17\n 995 |            Coordinate Descent solver.\n 996 | \n 997 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n 998 |            Multiplicative Update solver.\n 999 | \n1000 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n1001 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n1002 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n1003 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n1004 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n1005 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n1006 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros. Used only in 'mu' solver.\n1007 | \n1008 |         .. versionadded:: 0.19\n1009 | \n1010 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n1011 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n1012 | \n1013 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n1014 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n1015 | \n1016 |     alpha : float, default=0.0\n1017 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms. Set it to zero to have no\n1018 |         regularization. When using `alpha` instead of `alpha_W` and `alpha_H`, the\n1019 |         regularization terms are not scaled by the `n_features` (resp. `n_samples`)\n1020 |         factors for `W` (resp. `H`).\n1021 | \n1022 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1023 |             The `alpha` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in 1.2.\n1024 |             Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1025 | \n1026 |     alpha_W : float, default=0.0\n1027 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `W`. Set it to zero\n1028 |         (default) to have no regularization on `W`.\n1029 | \n1030 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1031 | \n1032 |     alpha_H : float or \"same\", default=\"same\"\n1033 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms of `H`. Set it to zero to\n1034 |         have no regularization on `H`. If \"same\" (default), it takes the same value as\n1035 |         `alpha_W`.\n1036 | \n1037 |         .. versionadded:: 1.0\n1038 | \n1039 |     l1_ratio : float, default=0.0\n1040 |         The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n1041 |         For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an elementwise L2 penalty\n1042 |         (aka Frobenius Norm).\n1043 |         For l1_ratio = 1 it is an elementwise L1 penalty.\n1044 |         For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n1045 | \n1046 |     regularization : {'both', 'components', 'transformation'}, default=None\n1047 |         Select whether the regularization affects the components (H), the\n1048 |         transformation (W), both or none of them.\n1049 | \n1050 |         .. deprecated:: 1.0\n1051 |             The `regularization` parameter is deprecated in 1.0 and will be removed in\n1052 |             1.2. Use `alpha_W` and `alpha_H` instead.\n1053 | \n1054 |     random_state : int, RandomState instance or None, default=None\n1055 |         Used for NMF initialisation (when ``init`` == 'nndsvdar' or\n1056 |         'random'), and in Coordinate Descent. Pass an int for reproducible\n1057 |         results across multiple function calls.\n1058 |         See :term:`Glossary <random_state>`.\n1059 | \n1060 |     verbose : int, default=0\n1061 |         The verbosity level.\n1062 | \n1063 |     shuffle : bool, default=False\n1064 |         If true, randomize the order of coordinates in the CD solver.\n1065 | \n1066 |     Returns\n1067 |     -------\n1068 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n1069 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1070 | \n1071 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n1072 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n1073 | \n1074 |     n_iter : int\n1075 |         Actual number of iterations.\n1076 | \n1077 |     Examples\n1078 |     --------\n1079 |     >>> import numpy as np\n1080 |     >>> X = np.array([[1,1], [2, 1], [3, 1.2], [4, 1], [5, 0.8], [6, 1]])\n1081 |     >>> from sklearn.decomposition import non_negative_factorization\n1082 |     >>> W, H, n_iter = non_negative_factorization(X, n_components=2,\n1083 |     ... init='random', random_state=0)\n1084 | \n1085 |     References\n1086 |     ----------\n1087 |     Cichocki, Andrzej, and P. H. A. N. Anh-Huy. \"Fast local algorithms for\n1088 |     large scale nonnegative matrix and tensor factorizations.\"\n1089 |     IEICE transactions on fundamentals of electronics, communications and\n1090 |     computer sciences 92.3: 708-721, 2009.\n1091 | \n1092 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n1093 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n1094 |     \"\"\"\n1095 |     X = check_array(X, accept_sparse=(\"csr\", \"csc\"), dtype=[np.float64, np.float32])\n1096 | \n1097 |     est = NMF(\n1098 |         n_components=n_components,\n1099 |         init=init,\n1100 |         solver=solver,\n1101 |         beta_loss=beta_loss,\n1102 |         tol=tol,\n1103 |         max_iter=max_iter,\n1104 |         random_state=random_state,\n1105 |         alpha=alpha,\n1106 |         alpha_W=alpha_W,\n1107 |         alpha_H=alpha_H,\n1108 |         l1_ratio=l1_ratio,\n1109 |         verbose=verbose,\n1110 |         shuffle=shuffle,\n1111 |         regularization=regularization,\n1112 |     )\n1113 | \n1114 |     with config_context(assume_finite=True):\n1115 |         W, H, n_iter = est._fit_transform(X, W=W, H=H, update_H=update_H)\n1116 | \n1117 |     return W, H, n_iter\n```\n\nFound function `benchmarks/bench_plot_nmf.py:_nls_subproblem`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n 49 | def _nls_subproblem(\n 50 |     X, W, H, tol, max_iter, alpha=0.0, l1_ratio=0.0, sigma=0.01, beta=0.1\n 51 | ):\n 52 |     \"\"\"Non-negative least square solver\n 53 |     Solves a non-negative least squares subproblem using the projected\n 54 |     gradient descent algorithm.\n 55 |     Parameters\n 56 |     ----------\n 57 |     X : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_features)\n 58 |         Constant matrix.\n 59 |     W : array-like, shape (n_samples, n_components)\n 60 |         Constant matrix.\n 61 |     H : array-like, shape (n_components, n_features)\n 62 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n 63 |     tol : float\n 64 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n 65 |     max_iter : int\n 66 |         Maximum number of iterations before timing out.\n 67 |     alpha : double, default: 0.\n 68 |         Constant that multiplies the regularization terms. Set it to zero to\n 69 |         have no regularization.\n 70 |     l1_ratio : double, default: 0.\n 71 |         The regularization mixing parameter, with 0 <= l1_ratio <= 1.\n 72 |         For l1_ratio = 0 the penalty is an L2 penalty.\n 73 |         For l1_ratio = 1 it is an L1 penalty.\n 74 |         For 0 < l1_ratio < 1, the penalty is a combination of L1 and L2.\n 75 |     sigma : float\n 76 |         Constant used in the sufficient decrease condition checked by the line\n 77 |         search.  Smaller values lead to a looser sufficient decrease condition,\n 78 |         thus reducing the time taken by the line search, but potentially\n 79 |         increasing the number of iterations of the projected gradient\n 80 |         procedure. 0.01 is a commonly used value in the optimization\n 81 |         literature.\n 82 |     beta : float\n 83 |         Factor by which the step size is decreased (resp. increased) until\n 84 |         (resp. as long as) the sufficient decrease condition is satisfied.\n 85 |         Larger values allow to find a better step size but lead to longer line\n 86 |         search. 0.1 is a commonly used value in the optimization literature.\n 87 |     Returns\n 88 |     -------\n 89 |     H : array-like, shape (n_components, n_features)\n 90 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n 91 |     grad : array-like, shape (n_components, n_features)\n 92 |         The gradient.\n 93 |     n_iter : int\n 94 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n 95 |     References\n 96 |     ----------\n 97 |     C.-J. Lin. Projected gradient methods for non-negative matrix\n 98 |     factorization. Neural Computation, 19(2007), 2756-2779.\n 99 |     https://www.csie.ntu.edu.tw/~cjlin/nmf/\n100 |     \"\"\"\n101 |     WtX = safe_sparse_dot(W.T, X)\n102 |     WtW = np.dot(W.T, W)\n103 | \n104 |     # values justified in the paper (alpha is renamed gamma)\n105 |     gamma = 1\n106 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n107 |         grad = np.dot(WtW, H) - WtX\n108 |         if alpha > 0 and l1_ratio == 1.0:\n109 |             grad += alpha\n110 |         elif alpha > 0:\n111 |             grad += alpha * (l1_ratio + (1 - l1_ratio) * H)\n112 | \n113 |         # The following multiplication with a boolean array is more than twice\n114 |         # as fast as indexing into grad.\n115 |         if _norm(grad * np.logical_or(grad < 0, H > 0)) < tol:\n116 |             break\n117 | \n118 |         Hp = H\n119 | \n120 |         for inner_iter in range(20):\n121 |             # Gradient step.\n122 |             Hn = H - gamma * grad\n123 |             # Projection step.\n124 |             Hn *= Hn > 0\n125 |             d = Hn - H\n126 |             gradd = np.dot(grad.ravel(), d.ravel())\n127 |             dQd = np.dot(np.dot(WtW, d).ravel(), d.ravel())\n128 |             suff_decr = (1 - sigma) * gradd + 0.5 * dQd < 0\n129 |             if inner_iter == 0:\n130 |                 decr_gamma = not suff_decr\n131 | \n132 |             if decr_gamma:\n133 |                 if suff_decr:\n134 |                     H = Hn\n135 |                     break\n136 |                 else:\n137 |                     gamma *= beta\n138 |             elif not suff_decr or (Hp == Hn).all():\n139 |                 H = Hp\n140 |                 break\n141 |             else:\n142 |                 gamma /= beta\n143 |                 Hp = Hn\n144 | \n145 |     if n_iter == max_iter:\n146 |         warnings.warn(\"Iteration limit reached in nls subproblem.\", ConvergenceWarning)\n147 | \n148 |     return H, grad, n_iter\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"Non-negative Matrix Factorization\", \"beta divergence\", \"Non-negative Matrix Factorization MiniBatchNMF beta divergence online implementation\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:_fit_multiplicative_update`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n725 | def _fit_multiplicative_update(\n726 |     X,\n727 |     W,\n728 |     H,\n729 |     beta_loss=\"frobenius\",\n730 |     max_iter=200,\n731 |     tol=1e-4,\n732 |     l1_reg_W=0,\n733 |     l1_reg_H=0,\n734 |     l2_reg_W=0,\n735 |     l2_reg_H=0,\n736 |     update_H=True,\n737 |     verbose=0,\n738 | ):\n739 |     \"\"\"Compute Non-negative Matrix Factorization with Multiplicative Update.\n740 | \n741 |     The objective function is _beta_divergence(X, WH) and is minimized with an\n742 |     alternating minimization of W and H. Each minimization is done with a\n743 |     Multiplicative Update.\n744 | \n745 |     Parameters\n746 |     ----------\n747 |     X : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_features)\n748 |         Constant input matrix.\n749 | \n750 |     W : array-like of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n751 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n752 | \n753 |     H : array-like of shape (n_components, n_features)\n754 |         Initial guess for the solution.\n755 | \n756 |     beta_loss : float or {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', \\\n757 |             'itakura-saito'}, default='frobenius'\n758 |         String must be in {'frobenius', 'kullback-leibler', 'itakura-saito'}.\n759 |         Beta divergence to be minimized, measuring the distance between X\n760 |         and the dot product WH. Note that values different from 'frobenius'\n761 |         (or 2) and 'kullback-leibler' (or 1) lead to significantly slower\n762 |         fits. Note that for beta_loss <= 0 (or 'itakura-saito'), the input\n763 |         matrix X cannot contain zeros.\n764 | \n765 |     max_iter : int, default=200\n766 |         Number of iterations.\n767 | \n768 |     tol : float, default=1e-4\n769 |         Tolerance of the stopping condition.\n770 | \n771 |     l1_reg_W : float, default=0.\n772 |         L1 regularization parameter for W.\n773 | \n774 |     l1_reg_H : float, default=0.\n775 |         L1 regularization parameter for H.\n776 | \n777 |     l2_reg_W : float, default=0.\n778 |         L2 regularization parameter for W.\n779 | \n780 |     l2_reg_H : float, default=0.\n781 |         L2 regularization parameter for H.\n782 | \n783 |     update_H : bool, default=True\n784 |         Set to True, both W and H will be estimated from initial guesses.\n785 |         Set to False, only W will be estimated.\n786 | \n787 |     verbose : int, default=0\n788 |         The verbosity level.\n789 | \n790 |     Returns\n791 |     -------\n792 |     W : ndarray of shape (n_samples, n_components)\n793 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n794 | \n795 |     H : ndarray of shape (n_components, n_features)\n796 |         Solution to the non-negative least squares problem.\n797 | \n798 |     n_iter : int\n799 |         The number of iterations done by the algorithm.\n800 | \n801 |     References\n802 |     ----------\n803 |     Fevotte, C., & Idier, J. (2011). Algorithms for nonnegative matrix\n804 |     factorization with the beta-divergence. Neural Computation, 23(9).\n805 |     \"\"\"\n806 |     start_time = time.time()\n807 | \n808 |     beta_loss = _beta_loss_to_float(beta_loss)\n809 | \n810 |     # gamma for Maximization-Minimization (MM) algorithm [Fevotte 2011]\n811 |     if beta_loss < 1:\n812 |         gamma = 1.0 / (2.0 - beta_loss)\n813 |     elif beta_loss > 2:\n814 |         gamma = 1.0 / (beta_loss - 1.0)\n815 |     else:\n816 |         gamma = 1.0\n817 | \n818 |     # used for the convergence criterion\n819 |     error_at_init = _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta_loss, square_root=True)\n820 |     previous_error = error_at_init\n821 | \n822 |     H_sum, HHt, XHt = None, None, None\n823 |     for n_iter in range(1, max_iter + 1):\n824 |         # update W\n825 |         # H_sum, HHt and XHt are saved and reused if not update_H\n826 |         delta_W, H_sum, HHt, XHt = _multiplicative_update_w(\n827 |             X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_W, l2_reg_W, gamma, H_sum, HHt, XHt, update_H\n828 |         )\n829 |         W *= delta_W\n830 | \n831 |         # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n832 |         if beta_loss < 1:\n833 |             W[W < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n834 | \n835 |         # update H\n836 |         if update_H:\n837 |             delta_H = _multiplicative_update_h(\n838 |                 X, W, H, beta_loss, l1_reg_H, l2_reg_H, gamma\n839 |             )\n840 |             H *= delta_H\n841 | \n842 |             # These values will be recomputed since H changed\n843 |             H_sum, HHt, XHt = None, None, None\n844 | \n845 |             # necessary for stability with beta_loss < 1\n846 |             if beta_loss <= 1:\n847 |                 H[H < np.finfo(np.float64).eps] = 0.0\n848 | \n849 |         # test convergence criterion every 10 iterations\n850 |         if tol > 0 and n_iter % 10 == 0:\n851 |             error = _beta_divergence(X, W, H, beta_loss, square_root=True)\n852 | \n853 |             if verbose:\n854 |                 iter_time = time.time()\n855 |                 print(\n856 |                     \"Epoch %02d reached after %.3f seconds, error: %f\"\n857 |                     % (n_iter, iter_time - start_time, error)\n858 |                 )\n859 | \n860 |             if (previous_error - error) / error_at_init < tol:\n861 |                 break\n862 |             previous_error = error\n863 | \n864 |     # do not print if we have already printed in the convergence test\n865 |     if verbose and (tol == 0 or n_iter % 10 != 0):\n866 |         end_time = time.time()\n867 |         print(\n868 |             \"Epoch %02d reached after %.3f seconds.\" % (n_iter, end_time - start_time)\n869 |         )\n870 | \n871 |     return W, H, n_iter\n```\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"NMF\", \"beta divergence\", \"Non-negative Matrix Factorization MiniBatchNMF beta divergence online implementation\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `sklearn/decomposition/_nmf.py:NMF`.\nSource: Match found for entity name `NMF`.\nJust show the structure of this class due to response length limitations:\n```\nclass NMF(_ClassNamePrefixFeaturesOutMixin, TransformerMixin, BaseEstimator):\n\n    def __init__(\n        self