reserved.\n 55 |     writeable : bool, optional\n 56 |         .. versionadded:: 1.12\n 57 | \n 58 |         If set to False, the returned array will always be readonly.\n 59 |         Otherwise it will be writable if the original array was. It\n 60 |         is advisable to set this to False if possible (see Notes).\n 61 | \n 62 |     Returns\n 63 |     -------\n 64 |     view : ndarray\n 65 | \n 66 |     See also\n 67 |     --------\n 68 |     broadcast_to: broadcast an array to a given shape.\n 69 |     reshape : reshape an array.\n 70 | \n 71 |     Notes\n 72 |     -----\n 73 |     ``as_strided`` creates a view into the array given the exact strides\n 74 |     and shape. This means it manipulates the internal data structure of\n 75 |     ndarray and, if done incorrectly, the array elements can point to\n 76 |     invalid memory and can corrupt results or crash your program.\n 77 |     It is advisable to always use the original ``x.strides`` when\n 78 |     calculating new strides to avoid reliance on a contiguous memory\n 79 |     layout.\n 80 | \n 81 |     Furthermore, arrays created with this function often contain self\n 82 |     overlapping memory, so that two elements are identical.\n 83 |     Vectorized write operations on such arrays will typically be\n 84 |     unpredictable. They may even give different results for small, large,\n 85 |     or transposed arrays.\n 86 |     Since writing to these arrays has to be tested and done with great\n 87 |     care, you may want to use ``writeable=False`` to avoid accidental write\n 88 |     operations.\n 89 | \n 90 |     For these reasons it is advisable to avoid ``as_strided`` when\n 91 |     possible.\n 92 |     \"\"\"\n 93 |     # first convert input to array, possibly keeping subclass\n 94 |     x = np.array(x, copy=False, subok=subok)\n 95 |     interface = dict(x.__array_interface__)\n 96 |     if shape is not None:\n 97 |         interface['shape'] = tuple(shape)\n 98 |     if strides is not None:\n 99 |         interface['strides'] = tuple(strides)\n100 | \n101 |     array = np.asarray(DummyArray(interface, base=x))\n102 |     # The route via `__interface__` does not preserve structured\n103 |     # dtypes. Since dtype should remain unchanged, we set it explicitly.\n104 |     array.dtype = x.dtype\n105 | \n106 |     view = _maybe_view_as_subclass(x, array)\n107 | \n108 |     if view.flags.writeable and not writeable:\n109 |         view.flags.writeable = False\n110 | \n111 |     return view\n```\n"}, {"content": "Based on the search results, the most relevant files for adding the `sliding_window` function are:\n\n- `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py`: Already contains the `as_strided` function, which can be used internally for the `sliding_window` implementation.\n- `numpy/lib/function_base.py`: Contains a variety of array operations and might be a suitable place for adding the `sliding_window` function.\n- `numpy/core/numeric.py`: Contains core numeric functions and could be a candidate for adding the `sliding_window` function to make it widely available.\n- `numpy/core/shape_base.py`: Contains functions for shape manipulation and could be a suitable location.\n- `numpy/lib/index_tricks.py`: Contains index-related utilities and might be a good place for the new function.\n\nWe will focus on `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py` and `numpy/lib/function_base.py` as they are closely related to the core functionality of `as_strided` and array manipulation.\n\nTo determine the exact location within these files, we can look for sections where similar utility functions are defined. Let's start with `numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py` to see if there are any specific sections or comments indicating where new utility functions should be added.\n\n<function=search_code_snippets>\n<parameter=search_terms>[\"utility functions\", \"array manipulation\"]</parameter>\n<parameter=file_path_or_pattern>numpy/lib/stride_tricks.py</parameter>\n", "role": "assistant", "tool_calls": null, "function_call": null}, {"role": "tool", "tool_call_id": "toolu_01", "name": "search_code_snippets", "content": "OBSERVATION:\nnumpy/lib/stride_tricks.py:as_strided\nnumpy/lib/stride_tricks.py:as_strided\n##Searching for term \"utility functions\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound class `numpy/core/code_generators/genapi.py:Function`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n103 | class Function:\n104 |     def __init__(self, name, return_type, args, doc=''):\n105 |         self.name = name\n106 |         self.return_type = _repl(return_type)\n107 |         self.args = args\n108 |         self.doc = doc\n109 | \n110 |     def _format_arg(self, typename, name):\n111 |         if typename.endswith('*'):\n112 |             return typename + name\n113 |         else:\n114 |             return typename + ' ' + name\n115 | \n116 |     def __str__(self):\n117 |         argstr = ', '.join([self._format_arg(*a) for a in self.args])\n118 |         if self.doc:\n119 |             doccomment = '/* %s */\\n' % self.doc\n120 |         else:\n121 |             doccomment = ''\n122 |         return '%s%s %s(%s)' % (doccomment, self.return_type, self.name, argstr)\n123 | \n124 |     def to_ReST(self):\n125 |         lines = ['::', '', '  ' + self.return_type]\n126 |         argstr = ',\\000'.join([self._format_arg(*a) for a in self.args])\n127 |         name = '  %s' % (self.name,)\n128 |         s = textwrap.wrap('(%s)' % (argstr,), width=72,\n129 |                           initial_indent=name,\n130 |                           subsequent_indent=' ' * (len(name)+1),\n131 |                           break_long_words=False)\n132 |         for l in s:\n133 |             lines.append(l.replace('\\000', ' ').rstrip())\n134 |         lines.append('')\n135 |         if self.doc:\n136 |             lines.append(textwrap.dedent(self.doc))\n137 |         return '\\n'.join(lines)\n138 | \n139 |     def api_hash(self):\n140 |         m = hashlib.md5()\n141 |         m.update(remove_whitespace(self.