plots if subplots is True.\n1482 | \n1483 |         See Also\n1484 |         --------\n1485 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of DataFrame using matplotlib / pylab.\n1486 | \n1487 |         Examples\n1488 |         --------\n1489 |         Draw an area plot based on basic business metrics:\n1490 | \n1491 |         .. plot::\n1492 |             :context: close-figs\n1493 | \n1494 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1495 |             ...     'sales': [3, 2, 3, 9, 10, 6],\n1496 |             ...     'signups': [5, 5, 6, 12, 14, 13],\n1497 |             ...     'visits': [20, 42, 28, 62, 81, 50],\n1498 |             ... }, index=pd.date_range(start='2018/01/01', end='2018/07/01',\n1499 |             ...                        freq='M'))\n1500 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area()\n1501 | \n1502 |         Area plots are stacked by default. To produce an unstacked plot,\n1503 |         pass ``stacked=False``:\n1504 | \n1505 |         .. plot::\n1506 |             :context: close-figs\n1507 | \n1508 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(stacked=False)\n1509 | \n1510 |         Draw an area plot for a single column:\n1511 | \n1512 |         .. plot::\n1513 |             :context: close-figs\n1514 | \n1515 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(y='sales')\n1516 | \n1517 |         Draw with a different `x`:\n1518 | \n1519 |         .. plot::\n1520 |             :context: close-figs\n1521 | \n1522 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({\n1523 |             ...     'sales': [3, 2, 3],\n1524 |             ...     'visits': [20, 42, 28],\n1525 |             ...     'day': [1, 2, 3],\n1526 |             ... })\n1527 |             >>> ax = df.plot.area(x='day')\n1528 |         \"\"\"\n1529 |         return self(kind=\"area\", x=x, y=y, **kwargs)\n```\n\nFound file `pandas/core/exchange/dataframe.py`.\nFound file `pandas/plotting/__init__.py`.\nSource: Retrieved entity using keyword search (bm25).\nHint: Use more detailed query to get the full content of some if needed.\n\n\n\n##Searching for term \"DataFrame.plot\", \"subplots=True\", \"DataFrame.plot subplots=True group columns subplot could accept a list of tuples\"...\n### Search Result:\nFound function `pandas/plotting/_core.py:PlotAccessor.pie`.\nSource: Retrieved code content using keyword search (bm25).\n```\n1531 | def pie(self, **kwargs):\n1532 |         \"\"\"\n1533 |         Generate a pie plot.\n1534 | \n1535 |         A pie plot is a proportional representation of the numerical data in a\n1536 |         column. This function wraps :meth:`matplotlib.pyplot.pie` for the\n1537 |         specified column. If no column reference is passed and\n1538 |         ``subplots=True`` a pie plot is drawn for each numerical column\n1539 |         independently.\n1540 | \n1541 |         Parameters\n1542 |         ----------\n1543 |         y : int or label, optional\n1544 |             Label or position of the column to plot.\n1545 |             If not provided, ``subplots=True`` argument must be passed.\n1546 |         **kwargs\n1547 |             Keyword arguments to pass on to :meth:`DataFrame.plot`.\n1548 | \n1549 |         Returns\n1550 |         -------\n1551 |         matplotlib.axes.Axes or np.ndarray of them\n1552 |             A NumPy array is returned when `subplots` is True.\n1553 | \n1554 |         See Also\n1555 |         --------\n1556 |         Series.plot.pie : Generate a pie plot for a Series.\n1557 |         DataFrame.plot : Make plots of a DataFrame.\n1558 | \n1559 |         Examples\n1560 |         --------\n1561 |         In the example below we have a DataFrame with the information about\n1562 |         planet's mass and radius. We pass the 'mass' column to the\n1563 |         pie function to get a pie plot.\n1564 | \n1565 |         .. plot::\n1566 |             :context: close-figs\n1567 | \n1568 |             >>> df = pd.DataFrame({'mass': [0.330, 4.87 , 5.97],\n1569 |             ...                    'radius': [2439.7, 6051.8, 6378.1]},\n1570 |             ...        