ses of datetime and carry metadata.\n135 | \n136 |     .. versionchanged:: 2.0.0\n137 |         The various numeric date/time attributes (:attr:`~DatetimeIndex.day`,\n138 |         :attr:`~DatetimeIndex.month`, :attr:`~DatetimeIndex.year` etc.) now have dtype\n139 |         ``int32``. Previously they had dtype ``int64``.\n140 | \n141 |     Parameters\n142 |     ----------\n143 |     data : array-like (1-dimensional)\n144 |         Datetime-like data to construct index with.\n145 |     freq : str or pandas offset object, optional\n146 |         One of pandas date offset strings or corresponding objects. The string\n147 |         'infer' can be passed in order to set the frequency of the index as the\n148 |         inferred frequency upon creation.\n149 |     tz : zoneinfo.ZoneInfo, pytz.timezone, dateutil.tz.tzfile, datetime.tzinfo or str\n150 |         Set the Timezone of the data.\n151 |     ambiguous : 'infer', bool-ndarray, 'NaT', default 'raise'\n152 |         When clocks moved backward due to DST, ambiguous times may arise.\n153 |         For example in Central European Time (UTC+01), when going from 03:00\n154 |         DST to 02:00 non-DST, 02:30:00 local time occurs both at 00:30:00 UTC\n155 |         and at 01:30:00 UTC. In such a situation, the `ambiguous` parameter\n156 |         dictates how ambiguous times should be handled.\n157 | \n158 |         - 'infer' will attempt to infer fall dst-transition hours based on\n159 |           order\n160 |         - bool-ndarray where True signifies a DST time, False signifies a\n161 |           non-DST time (note that this flag is only applicable for ambiguous\n162 |           times)\n163 |         - 'NaT' will return NaT where there are ambiguous times\n164 |         - 'raise' will raise a ValueError if there are ambiguous times.\n165 |     dayfirst : bool, default False\n166 |         If True, parse dates in `data` with the day first order.\n167 |     yearfirst : bool, default False\n168 |         If True parse da